ly label the basis state of the  symmetric and anti-symmetric subspaces  removing all redundancy we originally  had  this leads to the definition of the  occupation number representation  we write the state n1 n2 and so on  as the symmetrized or anti-symmetrized  state of a corresponding basis state  in v here i picked the ordered state for  convenience but from our discussion we  can pick  any permutation p alpha we want of this  state and it will lead to exactly the  same definition  of the occupation number representation  this all makes perfect sense because  we're dealing with identical particles  so it shouldn't matter which permutation  of the particles we use  there is a final detail in the  definition of the occupation number  representation  we will want to work with normalized  states so we'll write a normalization  constant in front here  a plus minus a few points before we move  on  first it immediately follows that the  total number of particles is equal to  the sum over all nk  second it could be that there are no  particles in a particular state so the  occupation numbers may be zero  all we have left to discuss are the  normalizations calculating them is  tedious and not a very enlightening  process  so i will not do it in this video i have  linked a reference in the description  where you can find the details or you  can try it out yourself  for our purposes all we need is the  answer if we start with bosons  then the occupation number  representation of the state  is given by the square root of these  factorials  and then the symmetrizer acting on the  tensor product basis state  the main question when looking at an  occupational number state for a boson  is how many particles occupy a given  state  this is an example of a boson occupation  number state  and what this means is that there are  two particles in single particle state  u1  so that n1 equals two then zero  particles in u2 so that n2 equals zero  three particles in u3 so n3 equals three  six particles in u4 so that n4 equals 6  and so on  in general we have an infinite number of  single particle states u  but only n particles this means that  many of the occupation numbers will be  zero  like in this example we had n2 equals  zero which meant that no particles were  in the single particle state  u2 to simplify notation rather than  writing the occupation number state like  this  with n1 and 2 and so on what is  typically done  is to write out only the occupation  numbers that are occupied and it is then  understood that the ones that are  emitted have zero occupation  so instead we will write n i and j and  so on  where n is the first occupation number  that is non-zero and j the second one  and so on  now let's turn to fermions in this case  the occupation number state  is given by the square root of n  factorial of the anti-symmetrizer  applied to the basis state of v  if all ui are different and it is zero  if any two ui are equal remember that a  totally anti-symmetric state that we  represent as a later determinant in the  position representation  vanishes if two particles are in the  same state this is reflected by this  expression and the main question for  fermions becomes whether state  is occupied or not the occupation  numbers can only take values of 0 or 1  so this is an example of a framionix  state and the corresponding occupations  are n1 equals 1  n2 equals 0 n3 equals 0 n4 equals 1  and so on just like in the case of  bosons we can write the occupation  number state like this  or we can also meet all the zeros and  simply write down the occupied states  like this  however given that for fermions  occupational numbers of occupied states  are all one  then rather than writing the ends we can  directly write down the corresponding  single particle state label  what this final expression is telling us  is that the states that have exactly one  fermion  are u i u j and so on  another critical point about fermions is  that the order is important this is  because fermions are described by  totally anti-semantic states which are  defined like this  this eta alpha is the parity of the  permutation  which you will remember from the video  on permutations that it is the parity of  the number of transpositions into which  you can write  out a permutation what this means is  that exchanging any two fermions  introduces a minus sign  and the ether is telling us that  exchanging pairs of fermions an odd  number of times introduces an overall  minus sign  and exchanging pairs of fermions an even  number of times leaves the state  unchanged  if we consider framinic state like this  and now exchange the two first occupied  states like this  then this action is represented by a  permutation that only exchanges the  first two particles and leaves the rest  invariant  which means it is a single transposition  so in this case eta is minus 1  so we need to introduce a minus sign in  this equality  so what have we accomplished with the  occupation number representation  let's compare the two representations in  this formulation  we specify the single particle state  that each particle occupies  and then after applying the symmetrizer  or anti-symmetrizer we end up with  n factorial terms in the calculations  containing all possible permutations of  the n particles in our system  the resulting expressions are there for  very long for example in the position  representation in terms of wave  functions for bosons they take the form  of a permanent  and for fermions in the form of isolated  determinant however  at the end of the calculation many of  these terms will cancel or be the same  because the indistinguishability of  identical quantum particles introduces a  lot of redundancy in this description  this is equivalent to the case in which  we labeled the balls in the example at  the very beginning of the video  that also proved to have a lot of  redundancy built in it  the occupation number representation  resolves this problem the relevant  quantity when we have identical  particles is not which particles  but instead how many particles occupy a  state  this makes perfect sense we have a  system of identical particles which are  indistinguishable  so assigning labels to them is  meaningless this is precisely what one  would expect for a compact formulation  of the physics of a system of identical  particles  and this is why the occupation of  representation is so powerful  the occupation number representation  leads to a very powerful formalism  to study systems of quantum identical  particles called second quantization  to learn more about second quantization  well you should check out the videos on  fox space  and on the creation and annihilation  operators that allow us to explore  that fox space so why would you want to  learn more about second quantization  it is the tool that allows us to study  quantum systems in which the number of  particles isn't fixed  for example in relativistic quantum  mechanics so called  quantum field theory or in quantum  statistical mechanics  if you liked the video or would like  send me suggestions for future videos  please subscribe
 

